            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Steven Weinberg's Early Life</strong><br>\nSteven Weinberg was born on May 3, 1933, in New York City. He developed an interest in physics at a young age, driven by a curiosity about the fundamental laws of nature. Weinberg attended the Bronx High School of Science, where he excelled in his studies. After high school, he went on to study physics at Cornell University and later received his Ph.D. from Princeton University.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contribution to Electroweak Unification</strong><br>\nSteven Weinberg is renowned for his work on the unification of the weak force and electromagnetic force, termed 'electroweak' unification. In 1967, he published a paper that proposed a model which predicted the existence of the 'W' and 'Z' bosons, particles that mediate the weak force. This work eventually led to his sharing the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1979.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Standard Model of Particle Physics</strong><br>\nWeinberg's contributions were fundamental in the establishment of the Standard Model of particle physics. The model combines electromagnetic, weak, and strong nuclear interactions into a unified framework. It has been a cornerstone of modern physics, providing predictions that have been confirmed by experiments to a high degree of accuracy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Cosmology and the Universe's Early Moments</strong><br>\nWeinberg also made significant contributions to cosmology, the study of the universe's origin and development. His book \"The First Three Minutes\" describes the events following the Big Bang. It became a popular science classic, making complex theories about the universe's early moments accessible to the general public.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"The First Three Minutes\"</strong><br>\nIn \"The First Three Minutes,\" Weinberg described the universe as it was very shortly after the Big Bang. His account brought the study of the early universe into the realm of experimental physics, suggesting ways in which early cosmic events could be tested by modern technology and observation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Advocate for Science and Rationality</strong><br>\nBeyond his scientific work, Weinberg was a vocal advocate for science and rational thinking. He wrote extensively on the topic, arguing for the importance of science in solving global problems and improving society. He believed deeply in the power of scientific inquiry to reveal the beauty of the natural world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
